WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:05.350
Hello, everyone! I'm Ms. Emma. Welcome to Online Grammar, level C one.

2
00:00:05.650 --> 00:00:09.849
Hi! I'm Visal. Let's learn together!

3
00:00:10.149 --> 00:00:17.155
Unit thirty-eight: Tense and Aspect in Reporting Texts. Today you will choose tenses in headlines and news reports.

4
00:00:17.455 --> 00:00:20.212
Here is our lesson map. Let's start!

5
00:00:21.167 --> 00:00:24.365
Part one: words. You will learn eight new words.

6
00:00:25.217 --> 00:00:26.369
Present perfect.

7
00:00:27.383 --> 00:00:30.370
Present perfect means a tense linking the past to now.

8
00:00:30.983 --> 00:00:32.588
The minister has resigned.

9
00:00:33.183 --> 00:00:36.091
Your turn. Say: Present perfect.

10
00:00:38.933 --> 00:00:40.109
Present perfect.

11
00:00:40.850 --> 00:00:41.921
Past simple.

12
00:00:42.917 --> 00:00:46.054
Past simple means a tense for finished past events.

13
00:00:46.650 --> 00:00:48.568
The minister resigned on Monday.

14
00:00:49.183 --> 00:00:52.021
Your turn. Say: Past simple.

15
00:00:54.867 --> 00:00:56.390
Past simple.

16
00:00:57.150 --> 00:00:58.325
Reporting verb.

17
00:00:59.317 --> 00:01:02.779
Reporting verb means a verb that introduces reported speech.

18
00:01:03.383 --> 00:01:04.698
He said, he added.

19
00:01:05.283 --> 00:01:08.133
Your turn. Say: Reporting verb.

20
00:01:10.967 --> 00:01:12.421
Reporting verb.

21
00:01:13.183 --> 00:01:14.161
Backshift.

22
00:01:15.150 --> 00:01:17.962
Backshift means moving a tense one step back.

23
00:01:18.550 --> 00:01:20.132
Backshift in reports.

24
00:01:20.717 --> 00:01:23.694
Your turn. Say: Backshift.

25
00:01:26.533 --> 00:01:27.476
Backshift.

26
00:01:28.217 --> 00:01:29.380
Time reference.

27
00:01:30.383 --> 00:01:32.685
Time reference means words that show when.

28
00:01:33.283 --> 00:01:35.260
Last week is a time reference.

29
00:01:35.850 --> 00:01:38.456
Your turn. Say: Time reference.

30
00:01:41.300 --> 00:01:42.545
Time reference.

31
00:01:43.283 --> 00:01:44.168
Still true.

32
00:01:45.183 --> 00:01:47.833
Still true means a fact that remains correct.

33
00:01:48.417 --> 00:01:49.998
A still true statement.

34
00:01:50.583 --> 00:01:53.561
Your turn. Say: Still true.

35
00:01:56.400 --> 00:01:57.784
Still true.

36
00:01:58.550 --> 00:01:59.760
News report.

37
00:02:00.750 --> 00:02:03.400
News report means a factual account of events.

38
00:02:03.983 --> 00:02:05.286
A news report.

39
00:02:05.883 --> 00:02:08.547
Your turn. Say: News report.

40
00:02:11.400 --> 00:02:12.598
News report.

41
00:02:13.350 --> 00:02:14.235
Headline.

42
00:02:15.250 --> 00:02:17.656
Headline means the title of a news story.

43
00:02:18.250 --> 00:02:20.215
A headline in the present tense.

44
00:02:20.817 --> 00:02:23.109
Your turn. Say: Headline.

45
00:02:25.967 --> 00:02:26.979
Headline.

46
00:02:27.767 --> 00:02:31.824
Part two: dialogue. Let's listen to a conversation.

47
00:02:32.683 --> 00:02:38.131
Visal, why do headlines say Minister resigns, but the story says The minister resigned?

48
00:02:39.217 --> 00:02:48.754
Headlines use the present simple to make events feel immediate and short. The report then switches to the past simple or present perfect for the full account.

49
00:02:49.850 --> 00:02:51.710
How do tenses work in the report?

50
00:02:52.817 --> 00:03:03.236
The first sentence often uses the present perfect to link the news to now: The minister has resigned. Details follow in the past simple: She announced it on Monday.

51
00:03:04.350 --> 00:03:06.117
And for reported speech?

52
00:03:07.217 --> 00:03:21.458
Reporting verbs are usually in the past, so we backshift: She said the economy was growing. But if the fact is still true, the present is acceptable. Time references like yesterday keep the story clear.

53
00:03:22.600 --> 00:03:24.298
Part three: grammar.

54
00:03:25.150 --> 00:03:30.865
Grammar focus: News tenses: headline present, lead present perfect, details past simple.

55
00:03:31.165 --> 00:03:32.561
Look at the pattern.

56
00:03:33.250 --> 00:03:36.248
The minister has resigned, officials confirmed today.

57
00:03:37.150 --> 00:03:39.161
She announced her decision on Monday.

58
00:03:40.050 --> 00:03:42.421
She said the economy was growing slowly.

59
00:03:43.367 --> 00:03:51.978
Headlines use the present simple. The lead often uses the present perfect. Details use the past simple, and reported speech usually backshifts.

60
00:03:52.717 --> 00:03:56.391
Be careful! Don't say: Yesterday the minister has resigned.

61
00:03:58.333 --> 00:04:00.612
Say: Yesterday the minister resigned.

62
00:04:00.912 --> 00:04:02.412
Use the past simple.

63
00:04:03.200 --> 00:04:06.038
Part four: reading. Read and listen.

64
00:04:06.833 --> 00:04:08.148
Reading the News.

65
00:04:08.448 --> 00:04:11.423
The headline reads: Minister resigns.

66
00:04:12.433 --> 00:04:14.816
The first line says the minister has resigned.

67
00:04:15.800 --> 00:04:18.902
She announced her decision at a press conference on Monday.

68
00:04:19.900 --> 00:04:22.410
She said that the economy was growing slowly.

69
00:04:23.400 --> 00:04:26.862
The tense changes as the story moves from news to detail.

70
00:04:27.967 --> 00:04:31.876
Part five: quiz. Five questions. Are you ready?

71
00:04:32.717 --> 00:04:35.006
Headline style: 'Minister blank'.

72
00:04:35.306 --> 00:04:39.459
A, resigns. B, resigned. C, has resigned.

73
00:04:43.000 --> 00:04:45.359
The answer is A: resigns.

74
00:04:46.517 --> 00:04:48.783
The news lead often uses the blank.

75
00:04:49.083 --> 00:04:54.223
A, past perfect continuous. B, present perfect. C, future perfect.

76
00:04:57.767 --> 00:05:00.149
The answer is B: present perfect.

77
00:05:01.283 --> 00:05:03.155
Detailed events use the blank.

78
00:05:03.455 --> 00:05:07.410
A, past simple. B, present simple. C, future.

79
00:05:10.967 --> 00:05:13.407
The answer is A: past simple.

80
00:05:14.550 --> 00:05:17.420
A reporting verb in the past usually causes blank.

81
00:05:17.720 --> 00:05:21.548
A, ellipsis. B, backshift. C, inversion.

82
00:05:25.100 --> 00:05:26.995
The answer is B: backshift.

83
00:05:28.150 --> 00:05:30.927
According to the reading, when did she announce her decision?

84
00:05:31.227 --> 00:05:35.009
A, on Monday. B, last year. C, tomorrow.

85
00:05:38.567 --> 00:05:40.566
The answer is A: on Monday.

86
00:05:41.767 --> 00:05:44.628
Part six: speaking. It's your turn to talk.

87
00:05:45.483 --> 00:05:50.165
Rewrite this headline-style sentence as full past simple: 'Minister resigns'.

88
00:05:58.950 --> 00:06:00.311
Now listen to Visal.

89
00:06:00.611 --> 00:06:11.007
The minister resigned yesterday. In the headline the present simple is used, but in the full story we use the past simple, because the event is finished.

90
00:06:11.307 --> 00:06:12.912
Excellent answer, Visal!

91
00:06:13.817 --> 00:06:21.868
Great work! Now you can use eight new words and news tenses: headline present, lead present perfect, details past simple.

92
00:06:22.168 --> 00:06:27.096
Today's value is practical wisdom. Judge ideas by how well they work in real life.

93
00:06:27.396 --> 00:06:29.967
See you in Unit thirty-nine. Goodbye!

94
00:06:30.267 --> 00:06:32.884
Thank you, Ms. Emma! Bye, everyone!

